<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maldives</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Maldives, Maldive Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Maldives, officially the Republic of Maldives, is an archipelagic country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, made up of 26 atolls. It has been inhabited for over 2,500 years. Its last Buddhist king, Dhovemi, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1153 (or 1193) and began a series of six Islamic dynasties. The Portuguese set up a garrison in 1558, and a local revolt later ended their presence. The islands became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1887, gained independence from the United Kingdom in 1965, and became a republic in 1968 after a referendum.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rajaraja I's naval campaigns captured Sri Lanka, the <b>Maldives</b> and the Malabar Coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tamil language and culture spread beyond Tamilakam, to the <b>Maldives</b>, Sri Lanka and some coasts of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1919 to 1945 it carried the anthem of the Korean exile government, from 1945 to 1948 South Korea's national anthem was sung to it, and the <b>Maldives</b> used it for their anthem until 1972.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a></li></ul></section>
